WEBVTT

00:00.470 --> 00:02.190
- [Instructor] This
polynomial division business

00:02.190 --> 00:03.890
is a little bit more fun than we expected.

00:03.890 --> 00:05.440
So let's keep going.

00:05.440 --> 00:07.580
So let's say that, I guess again,

00:07.580 --> 00:08.930
someone walks up to you in the street

00:08.930 --> 00:13.930
and says "What is x squared
plus one divided by x plus two."

00:14.270 --> 00:16.280
So pause this video and have a go at that.

00:16.280 --> 00:18.140
And I'll give you a
little bit of a warning.

00:18.140 --> 00:21.250
This one's a little bit more
involved than you might expect.

00:21.250 --> 00:23.620
All right, so there's two
ways to approach this.

00:23.620 --> 00:26.480
Either we can try to
re-express the numerator

00:26.480 --> 00:28.779
where it involves an x plus two somehow,

00:28.779 --> 00:32.100
or we could try to do
algebraic long division.

00:32.100 --> 00:33.480
So let me do the first way.

00:33.480 --> 00:35.010
So x squared plus one,

00:35.010 --> 00:38.410
it's not obvious that
you can factor it out.

00:38.410 --> 00:43.410
But can you write something
that has x plus two as a factor,

00:45.100 --> 00:47.920
and interestingly enough
has no first degree terms?

00:47.920 --> 00:50.310
'Cause we don't want
some first degree weird

00:50.310 --> 00:52.430
first degree terms sitting up there.

00:52.430 --> 00:54.610
And the best thing that
I could think of is,

00:54.610 --> 00:57.980
constructing a different of
squares using x plus two.

00:57.980 --> 01:02.980
So we know that x plus two
times x minus two is equal to

01:03.550 --> 01:06.620
x squared minus four.

01:06.620 --> 01:09.380
So what is we were to write
x squared minus four up here,

01:09.380 --> 01:13.900
and then we would just have to
add five to get to plus one.

01:13.900 --> 01:18.900
So what if we were to
write x squared minus four

01:19.000 --> 01:21.600
and then we write plus five.

01:21.600 --> 01:24.510
This expression and that
expression up there,

01:24.510 --> 01:26.730
those are completely equivalent.

01:26.730 --> 01:28.550
But why did I do that?

01:28.550 --> 01:33.380
Well, now I can write
x squared minus four as

01:33.380 --> 01:37.540
x plus two times x minus two.

01:37.540 --> 01:40.010
And so then I could rewrite
this entire expression

01:40.010 --> 01:45.010
as x plus two times x
minus two, all of that over

01:45.800 --> 01:50.800
x plus two plus five,
plus five over x plus two.

01:53.890 --> 01:58.160
And now as long as x does
not equal negative two,

01:58.160 --> 02:00.560
then we could divide the
numerator and the denominator

02:00.560 --> 02:02.500
by x plus two.

02:02.500 --> 02:07.500
And then we would be left
with x minus two plus five

02:08.430 --> 02:12.960
over x plus two, and I'll
put that little constraint,

02:12.960 --> 02:15.410
if I wanna say that this
expression is the same as that

02:15.410 --> 02:19.130
first expression, for x does not equal,

02:19.130 --> 02:21.540
for x not equaling negative two.

02:21.540 --> 02:24.860
And so here, we'd say "Hey!
X squared plus one divided by

02:24.860 --> 02:29.740
x plus two is x minus two,"
and then we have a remainder

02:29.740 --> 02:34.730
of five, remainder of five.

02:34.730 --> 02:37.750
Now let's do the same question,
or try to rewrite this

02:37.750 --> 02:39.200
using algebraic long division.

02:39.200 --> 02:40.080
We'll see that this is actually a

02:40.080 --> 02:41.820
little bit more straightforward.

02:41.820 --> 02:46.820
So we are going to divide
x plus two into x squared

02:49.531 --> 02:51.070
plus one.

02:51.070 --> 02:53.710
Now when I write things out I
like to be very careful with

02:53.710 --> 02:55.980
my, I guess you could say,
with my different places

02:55.980 --> 02:57.570
for the different degrees.

02:57.570 --> 03:00.050
So x squared plus one
has no first degree term,

03:00.050 --> 03:01.760
so I'm gonna write the one out here.

03:01.760 --> 03:05.270
So second degree, no first
degree term, and then

03:05.270 --> 03:06.170
we have a one, which is

03:06.170 --> 03:09.530
a zero degree term, or constant term.

03:09.530 --> 03:12.470
And so, we do the same
drill, how many times does

03:12.470 --> 03:13.720
x go into x squared.

03:13.720 --> 03:16.490
We're just looking at the
highest degree terms here.

03:16.490 --> 03:19.630
X goes into x squared x
times, that's first degree

03:19.630 --> 03:21.760
so I put it in the first degree column.

03:21.760 --> 03:26.260
X times two is two x.

03:26.260 --> 03:29.950
X times x is x squared.

03:29.950 --> 03:31.990
And now we wanna subtract.

03:31.990 --> 03:34.100
And so what is this gonna be equal to?

03:34.100 --> 03:36.550
We know the x squared's cancel out.

03:36.550 --> 03:38.470
And then I'm gonna be
subtracting negative two x

03:38.470 --> 03:42.570
from, you could do this
as plus zero x up here

03:42.570 --> 03:46.230
plus one, and so you're
left with negative two x.

03:46.230 --> 03:50.530
And then we bring down that one plus one.

03:50.530 --> 03:54.140
X goes into negative two
x, negative two times.

03:54.140 --> 03:56.810
Put that in the constant column.

03:56.810 --> 04:00.840
Negative two times two is negative four.

04:00.840 --> 04:05.840
And then negative two
times x is negative two x.

04:05.960 --> 04:08.050
Now we have to be very
careful here because we want

04:08.050 --> 04:10.870
to subtract the negative
two x minus four from

04:10.870 --> 04:13.230
the negative two x plus one.

04:13.230 --> 04:16.330
We could view it as this
or we could just distribute

04:16.330 --> 04:17.640
the negative sign.

04:17.640 --> 04:21.120
And then this will be
positive two x plus four.

04:21.120 --> 04:24.100
And then, the two x's, the two x

04:24.100 --> 04:25.950
and the negative two x cancels out.

04:25.950 --> 04:29.840
One plus four is five and
there's no obvious way

04:29.840 --> 04:32.520
of dividing x plus two
into five so we would call

04:32.520 --> 04:35.640
that the remainder,
exactly what we had before.

04:35.640 --> 04:37.590
When we divided with
algebraic long division,

04:37.590 --> 04:42.263
we got x minus two, x minus
two with a remainder of five.
